+## Reporting file system size
+
+By default, the NFS door reports a fixed total size of 1 EiB and the space used by all files in the
+namespace, which makes the output of tools like `df` of little use. The door can instead report the
+capacity of the pools or of a space reservation, as selected by `nfs.capacity.source`:
+
+| Value | Reported size | Reported free space |
+|---|---|---|
+| `namespace` (default) | 1 EiB | 1 EiB minus the size of all files in the namespace |
+| `pools` | Sum of the total space of all online pools | Sum of the free and removable space of those pools |
+| `reservation` | Size of the space reservation | Space neither used nor allocated in the reservation |
+
+For example, to report the space reservation with the token 1234:
+
+```ini
+nfs.capacity.source = reservation
+nfs.capacity.reservation = 1234
+```
+
+As NFS clients don't tell the door which export a request is for, the door can't use the
+`WriteToken` tag of a directory to find the space reservation, and a single reservation is reported
+for all exports of a door. If `nfs.capacity.source` is `reservation` and
+`nfs.capacity.reservation` is empty, the door fails to start. Sites that need different values
+for different exports have to run separate doors.
+
+With `pools`, files that exist only on tape aren't counted, so on tape-backed instances the reported
+used space may be much smaller than the size of all files.
+
+The number of files is always taken from the namespace. If the pools or the space reservation
+can't be looked up, the door reports the default values. The values are cached for
+`nfs.fs-stat-cache.time`, which may need to be lowered for clients to see changes promptly.
+
+Both properties default to `dcache.capacity.source` and `dcache.capacity.reservation`, which the
+xrootd door uses as well.
+
+
